# Read-only component props (Sonar S6759)

All React components under `src/components/` must satisfy Sonar rule **typescript:S6759**: props are read-only and must not be mutated inside the component.

## Required pattern

1. Define a dedicated exported props type wrapped in `Readonly<...>`.
2. Prefer a single `props` parameter typed as `Readonly<YourProps>` (satisfies Sonar **typescript:S6759**; avoids destructuring in the signature).

```tsx
export type MyComponentProps = Readonly<{
  label: string;
  onClick?: () => void;
}>;

export function MyComponent(props: Readonly<MyComponentProps>) {
  const { label, onClick } = props;
  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;
}
```

```tsx
export type TextInputProps = Readonly<
  InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement> & {
    label?: ReactNode;
    labelClassName?: string;
    wrapperClassName?: string;
  }
>;

export function TextInput(props: Readonly<TextInputProps>) {
  const { label, className, type = "text", ...rest } = props;
  // Renders label + input when `label` is provided
}
```

```tsx
export type EmailInputProps = Readonly<
  Omit<InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement>, "type">
>;

export function EmailInput(props: Readonly<EmailInputProps>) {
  const { className, autoComplete = "email", ...rest } = props;
  return (
    <input
      type="email"
      autoComplete={autoComplete}
      className={className}
      {...rest}
    />
  );
}
```

## Linting

- **SonarLint** (`typescript:S6759`) is the source of truth — keep it enabled in `.vscode/settings.json`.
- Run **SonarLint: Analyze current file** on new components before committing.

## Do not

- Use a mutable props type without `Readonly`.
- Mutate `props` or destructured prop values (e.g. `props.className = "..."`).
- Re-export props types from another component file—keep each component standalone with its own `Readonly` props type.

## Reference

`src/app/layout.tsx` and existing files in `src/components/` follow this convention.
